WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.634
Hi, I'm Pim!

00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:06.637
A new plugin promises cheaper Claude Code and Codex without touching the agent.

00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:09.717
Here is where it sits, and what it can cut.

00:00:11.325 --> 00:00:14.500
Every agent turn sends the tools, the system prompt, the task

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:15.654
and the whole history.

00:00:15.654 --> 00:00:18.829
The next turn sends it all again, plus a little more.

00:00:19.249 --> 00:00:21.262
The cache makes the repeat cheap, not free.

00:00:21.262 --> 00:00:24.281
The old part is read at a tenth of the price, the

00:00:24.281 --> 00:00:25.287
new tail is written.

00:00:25.707 --> 00:00:29.725
So a tool the agent never calls is still paid for, on every turn.

00:00:29.725 --> 00:00:31.733
Remove it, and every row gets shorter.

00:00:32.154 --> 00:00:33.098
One case hurts.

00:00:33.098 --> 00:00:36.873
Resume a conversation after its tools changed, and the whole history is

00:00:36.873 --> 00:00:38.446
written to the cache again.

00:00:39.992 --> 00:00:43.673
The authors' picture: each finished session teaches the plugin one thing.

00:00:44.053 --> 00:00:47.488
Remember how the repo works, skip tools never used, and step in

00:00:47.488 --> 00:00:48.633
when a turn loops.

00:00:50.142 --> 00:00:51.706
It is not a proxy.

00:00:51.706 --> 00:00:52.645
Nothing rewrites requests.

00:00:52.645 --> 00:00:55.774
At session start, a hook adds a few learned facts.

00:00:56.174 --> 00:01:00.086
After each tool call, a loop guard watches for repeated failures and

00:01:00.086 --> 00:01:01.716
may attach a short redirect.

00:01:02.116 --> 00:01:05.133
When the session ends, it reads the agent's own log and

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:06.231
updates what it knows.

00:01:06.631 --> 00:01:10.074
Unused tools go into a deny list in the repo's settings, seen

00:01:10.074 --> 00:01:11.508
only by the next session.

00:01:13.325 --> 00:01:16.949
In the paper's harness, a stuck run was restarted with a

00:01:16.949 --> 00:01:18.267
fresh conversation, then aborted.

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.914
The plugin never does that.

00:01:21.225 --> 00:01:24.525
On the benchmark, a judge masked nineteen tools on Claude Code.

00:01:24.945 --> 00:01:28.732
The plugin protects eleven of them, because a person asks questions,

00:01:28.732 --> 00:01:30.109
plans, and spawns sub-agents.

00:01:30.109 --> 00:01:31.486
It may prune eight.

00:01:33.175 --> 00:01:35.782
I captured what a current Claude Code sends.

00:01:35.782 --> 00:01:37.412
It already defers most tools.

00:01:37.412 --> 00:01:40.020
The plugin's list still drops two big ones.

00:01:41.658 --> 00:01:45.701
On the benchmark, the full stack was cheaper in every repository and

00:01:45.701 --> 00:01:47.723
setting, with task success within noise.

00:01:49.358 --> 00:01:53.620
But the measured stack had learned facts switched on, the wider mask, and restarts.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.598
The plugin ships facts off, prunes fewer tools, and only nudges.

00:01:59.892 --> 00:02:02.795
Worth installing in the repos you use every day.

00:02:02.795 --> 00:02:06.022
Then trust its own cost ledger, not the paper's headline.

00:02:07.692 --> 00:02:08.890
Hooks, not a proxy.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:10.088
Careful with the cache.

00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:11.886
A smaller cut than the headline.

00:02:11.886 --> 00:02:12.785
Thanks for watching!

00:02:13.205 --> 00:02:15.237
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:15.237 --> 00:02:15.817
I'm Pim.

00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:16.108
Bye!
